13:26:20 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I'll just disagree with that one directly. It's not "common sense"—it's a stereotype, and not an accurate one. Butchery actually requires a lot of specialized knowledge: understanding animal anatomy in detail, food safety and sanitation, how different muscles behave when cooked, aging and preservation techniques, knife skills that take years to develop, and often real business acumen if they're running or managing a shop. None of that is rocket science, sure, but "not rocket science" describes basically every job that isn't literally aerospace engineering. It's a low bar that doesn't actually tell you anything. The "common sense" framing is worth questioning too—a lot of stereotypes get passed around as obvious truths without much scrutiny. Manual trades in general get stereotyped as less intellectually demanding than white-collar work, even when they require serious technical skill and problem-solving. That's more about cultural bias around class and type of work than any real assessment of intelligence. You clearly enjoy talking to this guy and find him engaging—that's actually pretty good evidence against the stereotype, at least in his case. Trust that observation over the generic assumption.
