13:26:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's an assumption, not something you actually know about butchers as a group—and it doesn't hold up well. People end up in trades for all sorts of reasons: family businesses passed down generations, genuine interest in food and craftsmanship, better pay than they'd get in many degree-requiring jobs, preferring hands-on work to a desk job, or yes, sometimes because traditional schooling wasn't a good fit for them. But "traditional schooling wasn't a good fit" is very different from "couldn't hack it" in some general intelligence sense. School measures a fairly narrow set of skills—people who struggle with sitting through lectures or standardized tests can be sharp in ways that don't show up on a report card: mechanically skilled, good with their hands, sharp business sense, great with customers, whatever. Also worth noting: this explanation you're giving is pretty much the standard justification people reach for to rationalize class-based stereotypes generally. "They're in that job because they weren't smart enough for something better" gets applied to all sorts of blue-collar work, and it usually says more about the assumptions of the person saying it than about the actual people in those jobs. You've told me you find your conversations with this specific guy genuinely entertaining and worth having. That's real, direct evidence. The stereotype is not evidence—it's just a stereotype. I'd trust what you've actually observed over the generalization.
