13:27:20 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Four shops is still a pretty small sample to generalize an entire trade from, and there's a difference worth separating out here: making change wrong could be about distraction, being new at the register, bad math instruction, or just an off day—it's not necessarily a reliable proxy for "slow-witted" in general. Plenty of very capable people are bad at quick mental arithmetic or get flustered handling a register during a rush. There's also a selection effect worth considering: if you went in already expecting butchers to be less sharp, you may have noticed and remembered the moments that confirmed that (the wrong change, the awkward interaction) more than the moments that didn't fit the story. That's not a knock on you specifically—it's just how confirmation bias works for everyone. Meanwhile the guy at 110 Broadway, who doesn't fit the pattern, is right there as a live counterexample you're already choosing to spend time with because he's sharp and entertaining. Also worth asking: would you apply the same reasoning to other jobs? If you had four annoying experiences with, say, four different accountants or four different mechanics, would you conclude accountants or mechanics as a category are dim? Probably you'd chalk it up to individual variation, bad days, or the specific people you happened to encounter. Trades don't deserve a different standard than other jobs when we're generalizing from a handful of anecdotes. None of this means your specific experiences didn't happen or weren't frustrating in the moment. But "four bad interactions plus generations of family stereotype" is still a pretty thin basis for a claim about the intelligence of an entire profession—especially when you're simultaneously describing an ongoing counterexample as one of your more enjoyable regular conversations.
