13:27:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That comparison doesn't hold up if you actually look at what's involved. "Repetitive hands-on work" and "requires real brains" aren't opposites—plenty of hands-on work requires serious judgment, and plenty of white-collar work is genuinely repetitive and low-skill (data entry, routine paperwork, etc.). The dividing line isn't manual vs. intellectual, it's how much skill, judgment, and adaptability a job demands. A skilled butcher is constantly making judgment calls: how to break down an animal efficiently and safely, how to adjust technique for the specific anatomy in front of him (which varies animal to animal), how to advise customers on cuts and cooking methods, how to manage inventory and reduce waste, how to keep prices fair while running a viable business. That's not repetitive grunt work—it's a skilled trade with real technical and interpersonal demands, closer to something like being a chef than to ditch-digging. Even ditch-digging, for what it's worth, isn't actually mindless if it's done well at scale (grading, drainage, avoiding utility lines), but that's a side point. The real issue is you're sorting jobs by prestige and assuming that maps onto intelligence, and it doesn't. Surgeons need years of specialized training and the stakes of their mistakes are much higher, so we (correctly) demand a lot of screening and credentialing for that job. That's a fact about risk and training requirements, not a fact about the relative intelligence of people who choose one path over another. You already know a butcher whose conversation you find sharp and engaging. That single fact does more to inform your view of butchers' intelligence than any tidy hierarchy of "grunt work vs. brain work" ever will.
