13:29:42 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Those actually aren't the same claim, and the gap between them matters. A job's formal complexity rating measures the *minimum* cognitive floor needed to perform the core tasks adequately — not what the people doing it actually use day to day, and not their capability ceiling. Someone can be in a job that doesn't strictly require deep abstract reasoning and still bring plenty of brainpower to it — solving problems creatively, picking up nuance, being good with customers, running the business side well, adapting technique on the fly. The job not *requiring* much doesn't mean the person doing it isn't *using* much, or isn't *capable* of much. Quick example to test the logic: many jobs rated as "low complexity" — cashier, retail associate, delivery driver — are done by college students, career-changers, people between other jobs, or people who are quite sharp but for whatever reason are in a transitional or non-credentialed role. Nobody would say a philosophy grad working retail for a year has a lower IQ than one working at a law firm, even though the job complexity ratings would differ wildly. The job and the person are just not the same variable. Also worth noting: plenty of high-complexity-rated jobs on paper (say, some corporate middle-management roles) involve people mostly doing routine, low-thought tasks day to day despite the formal rating, while some "low-complexity" trades involve constant real-time problem solving that doesn't show up well in a static rating system, because the ratings tend to reward credential requirements and abstract/symbolic tasks over adaptive physical and interpersonal skill. So: job complexity ratings are a real thing, and it's fine to say butchery scores lower on that specific measured axis. But collapsing that into "the people doing it are less intelligent" skips a step that isn't actually justified by the data — it's an inference, not something the numbers say directly.
