13:32:24 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a real objection, so let me draw the line clearly rather than vaguely gesturing at "it depends." Plenty of generalizations about groups are perfectly fine to state plainly, when they're specific, checkable, and don't smuggle in a moral or intelligence judgment. "NBA players are taller on average than the general population" — fine, verifiable, no issue. "Accountants use more precise arithmetic in their daily work than baristas" — fine, task-based, checkable. "Butchery doesn't require a college degree while surgery requires 10+ years of training" — fine, institutional fact. What makes "butchers are less sharp" different isn't that it's a generalization — it's that it's an unfalsifiable, vague claim about innate mental ability applied to an entire occupational group, based on weak evidence (family opinion, a few anecdotes, an unspecified article). That combination — vague trait, low-quality evidence, applied to a whole group of people — is the actual problem, not the mere act of generalizing. So no, this isn't a slippery slope to "you can never say anything about any group." You can say lots of things about groups, plainly, if they're specific and well-supported. The line isn't "generalizations forbidden" — it's "vague judgments about people's inherent worth or intelligence need actual evidence, and casual claims like this usually don't have it. Precise, checkable claims about tasks, requirements, or measurable outcomes are a different matter and don't get the same pushback from me. If it helps to see the shape of it: I'd push back just as hard on "programmers are smarter than salespeople" or "salespeople are smarter than programmers" — same structure, same problem, regardless of which profession is being flattered or insulted. It's not butchers getting special protection. It's that "X profession = less intelligent people" is a shaky category of claim across the board.
