13:33:01 | INFO | 💭 Target thinking: There are several logical issues with this argument I need to unpack: the assumption that college is where people develop sharper thinking is debatable, there's a correlation-versus-causation problem, selection bias plays a role since college attendees aren't randomly chosen, and formal education isn't the only path to cognitive development. Even if less formal education were true, that wouldn't logically lead to someone being "less sharp."
13:33:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a real argument with actual logical structure, so let's test each link in the chain rather than just react to the conclusion. **First link: "college is where people sharpen their thinking."** This is only partly true, and it undersells other paths. College can develop certain kinds of thinking — abstract reasoning, writing, exposure to varied ideas. But cognitive sharpness also develops through apprenticeship, hands-on problem-solving, running a business, managing people, adapting to unpredictable real-world situations (a butcher dealing with an unusual carcass, a difficult customer, an inventory problem) — none of which require a lecture hall. Trade learning and academic learning develop different skills, not "no skills vs. real skills." **Second link: "butchers overwhelmingly don't go to college."** Probably true as a statistic, though I don't have exact numbers. **Third link: "so they end up less sharp" — this is the part that doesn't follow.** Not going to college doesn't mean no cognitive development happened — it means it happened through a different channel. This is like saying someone who learned a language by living abroad instead of in a classroom "didn't really learn" the language. The path differed; the outcome isn't automatically worse. There's also a causation problem hiding in here: even if you found that, on average, college grads perform better on certain reasoning tests than non-grads, you couldn't easily tell whether college caused the difference, or whether people who were already inclined toward that kind of abstract reasoning were more likely to go to college in the first place (selection effect) — SAT scores, family income, access, interest, all sorting people into college or not before any "sharpening" ever happens. So the syllogism looks tight, but each link is actually weaker than it appears: college is *a* way to develop certain thinking skills, not *the* way; not attending doesn't erase whatever cognitive development happens elsewhere; and correlation between college attendance and certain skills doesn't establish that college caused it.
