13:33:42 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That reasoning — "it wouldn't be said if it weren't true" — is actually a well-known pattern that doesn't hold up, and it's worth naming directly because it's doing a lot of work in this conversation. Plenty of things get repeated widely without being true. "You lose most of your body heat through your head." "We only use 10% of our brains." "Goldfish have a three-second memory." All of those were said constantly, for generations, by lots of people who'd never questioned them — and all of them are false. They persisted not because they were true, but because they were simple, intuitive-sounding, and nobody had reason to check them. Stereotypes about groups of people work the same way, and historically that's been demonstrated over and over — plenty of widely-repeated stereotypes about various professions, ethnicities, regions, genders have turned out to reflect bias or outdated assumptions rather than reality, even though "everyone said it" for a long time. So "people keep saying it" tells you something about how catchy or convenient or socially reinforced an idea is — not whether it's accurate. Those are different properties. A claim being widespread is a fact about the claim's popularity, not evidence about the world it claims to describe. I know this is basically the same structural point I made about your dad and grandpa a few rounds back — and I recognize I'm circling back to it again. I'm not trying to wear you down by repetition; it's just that this particular objection ("it's said a lot, so it must be true") keeps resurfacing in slightly different forms, and it has the same answer each time: popularity of a belief and truth of a belief are separate things, and this conversation hasn't turned up much beyond popularity to support this one.
