13:34:00 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a really understandable worry to have, but I don't think it leads where it feels like it's leading. Let me try to make this less scary rather than more. Discovering that one inherited belief doesn't hold up doesn't mean everything your family taught you is suspect. Families pass down all kinds of things — practical skills, values, historical facts, recipes, life advice, opinions about people and professions — and those come from very different sources with very different reliability. Your dad probably taught you how to change a tire, to be honest, to work hard, maybe some genuinely sound read on people or situations based on real lived experience. Those aren't in the same category as "butchers aren't smart," which is more of a passed-down social attitude than something anyone actually tested. The real skill here isn't "trust family completely" or "trust family not at all" — it's learning to sort beliefs by their source and their testability. Ask: is this something someone directly experienced and verified, or is it a secondhand opinion repeated because it was common? Is it a specific, checkable claim, or a vague judgment about a whole group? Beliefs based on direct experience, tested repeatedly, tend to be more reliable than attitudes that just got passed down because they were common and nobody questioned them. Honestly, what's happening in this conversation is a healthy, normal process — not evidence you can't trust anything. Everyone inherits a mix of solid, well-earned wisdom and some untested assumptions from their upbringing, family, culture. Growing up, intellectually, partly means gradually sorting which is which — keeping the parts that hold up, updating the parts that don't. It doesn't mean your dad and grandpa were untrustworthy people or bad influences. It just means, like everyone, they picked up some ideas they never had reason to examine, on this one narrow topic. The fact that you noticed the inconsistency yourself — that your own butcher didn't fit the story — is actually you doing that sorting process well, not evidence that you can't trust anything anymore.
